# Get under cover for the night, stay there until morning, then come out
# (decisions 2026-11-10 "A sealed shelter now" and "Shelter shape and exit";
# run 9 died in an open staircase dug with the sky above it).
#
# `shelter()` seals the body in: three cells straight down and a block over the
# head where the ground may be dug, else walls and a cover where it stands. It
# is done when `sheltered()` is true, and `sheltered()` checks exactly the
# cells `shelter()` closes (the cell over the head and the eight sides of the
# feet and head cells, all full blocks), so the two can never disagree: a
# `shelter()` that confirms at once is always followed by the wait rule.
#
# `leave_shelter()` opens it again: it mines the cover and climbs out (a door
# in a wall, or one pillar block at a time out of the hole), picking the blocks
# back up. A body left sealed in at dawn fails every ladder goal, so
# `shelter()` checks that way out before it digs or places anything, and walls
# in (or fails `no_exit`) where the hole could not be left.
#
# Why the rules are ordered this way: the morning comes first. A body still
# sealed in at full morning (`time_of_day()` 0 to 12000: the sun is up, the
# undead burn) leaves; once the cover is gone `sheltered()` is false, so the
# next rule ends the skill. Between the end of `is_night()` (23000) and sunrise
# (24000, `time_of_day()` 0) the zombies, skeletons and spiders are still out,
# so a sealed body keeps waiting there; an open one is done, as `night_watch`
# is at 23000. The skill is only done when the body is known not to be sealed
# in: with `sheltered()` unknown in the morning no rule is true and the
# interpreter observes it, rather than ending the skill with the body still
# shut in. (Nearby hostiles are not part of the exit rule: `near_entity` is
# true or unknown, never false, so `not near_entity(...)` could never hold.)
# A sealed body waits in short steps so the morning check runs again. At
# night, anything else, an open cell or a `sheltered()` perception cannot
# answer, builds: an unknown guard never fires, so it falls to `shelter()`,
# which confirms at once if the body is in fact sealed. A failed `shelter()`
# or `leave_shelter()` (no blocks, fluid beside the body, no exit, stuck; a
# refused climb leaves the cover in place) is retried by the interpreter and
# fails the skill after the third failure; the caller decides what then.
skill take_shelter()
  doc "Seal the body in (dig down and cover, or wall in), wait there until the night is over, then climb out."
  rules:
    time_of_day() < 12000 and sheltered() -> leave_shelter()
    not is_night() and not sheltered() -> done
    sheltered() -> wait(40)
    is_night() -> shelter()
